\f1\fs22 Three hours later, Agent Jones was the good kind of angry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Earlier, the briefing with the new police Commissioner had gone as expected, pleasantries and all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What is your name, Agent?\'94 Gerry asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ajent Jones,\'94 came the brisk reply.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry, missing the nasal faux-French, pressed, \'93No, I mean your given name. The one your parents gave you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ajent,\'94 Agent Ajent Jones reiterated, careful not to sound testy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Agent?\'94 Gerry repeated, refusing to imitate the accent. \'93Your mother called you Agent Jones?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i Ar-zhant,
\f1\i0 \'94 Jones emphasised.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Very well, Special Agent 
\f2\i Ar-zhant
\f1\i0  Jones. Let\'92s continue.\'94 Gerry was all declarative, colonial certainty.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The briefing went well enough. Gerry, annoyingly sharp, questioned how Anthony \'93Chanta\'94 Chantara could be responsible for all the charges, not to mention the endless starring role in the confessions episodes of 
\f2\i Curb Your Individualism
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Need to know,\'94 Agent Jones said, expecting a fight. Hardman just nodded, jaw set. Suspicious.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry, for his part, acted unconcerned, or truly ignorant. Or maybe he was hiding something. Very suspicious.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time Agent Jones retreated to his own office, he was the good kind of angry. Not the kind of angry that required ritual chanting: 
\f2\i I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the Company\'92s will. In the name of stability I destabilise. In the name of order I deliver chaos. In the name of life I bring death.
\f1\i0  This was the kind of anger he could manage with a good doodle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones sat at his desk. Out came the art pad and a new box of HB pencils. He lay the pad flat, smoothed the page, tumbled the pencils onto the desk. Grabbed a 2B like he was strangling a duck.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Doodling helped. It let out the voices, let ideas sneak past, let the muscle under his skin uncoil. He loosened his black tie, and he drew: spiderwebs in the corners, ornate stars down the left, kittens at the bottom\'97some fucking and others stabbing each other with sharpened bed springs. He entered a meditative state. His angers dissolved like jagged shards of toffee in warm water. Muscles slackened. Bones became scaffolding. For a while, he ceased to exist.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When he returned, the middle of the page read:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry Hardman is a spy
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not that Jones knew that Hardman was a PAPER agent. Institutional stupidity, on a pure \'93need-to-know\'94 basis, deemed Agent Jones needn\'92t know Gerry Hardman was a fellow agent. And so he didn\'92t. Had it been decided he did need to know, he would have known, and then wouldn\'92t need to wonder what to do about Gerry\'97because he\'92d already know, and he\'92d know he knew, and that would be that. But as it was, Agent Jones didn\'92t know what he should know, or even that he needed to know it, which wouldn\'92t matter\'97except now he did know, and still had no idea what to do.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was the good kind of angry again. With something like inspiration, he wrote Gerry Hardman over and over, each time heavier. He snapped pencil after pencil, flinging broken tips at the wall, until he was writing:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>G. Hardman
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Over and over, darker and darker. Then the demon of subconscious bureaucracy possessed him, and the syllables blurred:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>G hard man\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>G hard man\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He stopped. Absolutely still. Eyebrows arched, sweat on his upper lip, tears stinging. Then, with eyes afire, he pressed the pencil one last time. With ritual weight, he wrote:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jihad Man
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>and then all his anger strangely emptied out. Agent Jones leaned back, closed his eyes, and wondered if he had just solved a riddle, or written his own obituary.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

